foreign  this documentary by Russian State media  Praises a private military company  called Wagner the paramilitary group  operating in Ukraine has been getting a  lot of positive coverage  all this despite private military  organizations being illegal in Russia  just before the invasion of Ukraine the  Russian president denied any ties  Kiev has accused Wagner of war crimes  now as Moscow loses ground in Ukraine  the company is gaining prominence  news reports and social media accounts  show Wagner's men leading attacks in  eastern Ukraine tapping prisoners to  fight and sending threatening messages  to the kremlin's opponents here's how  the secretive company has come to take  on a semi-official role in Ukraine  formed in 2014 to fight in Ukraine back  then Wagner has since operated in Syria  and several African countries  since the spring it's been indispensable  for moscow's invasion of Ukraine that's  according to Ukrainian commanders who  detected its presence early on after  Russia's main offensive stalled in May  Wagner managed to take the town of  papasana Paving the way for the Russian  army to take several strategic cities  recently the group has been fighting for  the eastern city of bakmut  version  as Wagner slowly advances toward the  city Russian propaganda has been  amplifying its importance anticipating  that President Vladimir Putin could tout  a rare Victory after a series of losses  and setbacks  but Wagner Services come at a cost the  group has been losing about 10 to 15  percent of its fighters on an average  mission in Ukraine as dead or wounded  that's according to an estimate by a  former Ukrainian intelligence official  Wagner's Fighters are paid better than  the regular Russian troops with an  advertised starting monthly pay of  around three thousand five hundred  dollars compared to two thousand seven  hundred dollars that Moscow pays its  soldiers fighting in Ukraine  and recently the company has resorted to  unconventional measures to find new  recruits  in September a video circulating online  showed a man recruiting inmates for  Wagner in a Russian prison the news  agency storyfull was able to confirm the  location  a social media account affiliated with  Wagner identified the man as yevgeny  pregosian Wagner's owner and leader  pregosian has said he has visited  several penal colonies but he declined  to answer our questions on the  recruitment of inmates and Wagner's role  in the war  enlisting prisoners comes as Moscow is  trying to balance its highly unpopular  mobilization efforts with its need for  troops  Russian human rights activists estimated  that at least 15 000 inmates had been  recruited to fight in Ukraine by  mid-october  recruiting inmates has another Advantage  as the official troops morale falters  consequences for refusing to fight are  more severe for them  that message was reinforced in November  a wagner-linked social media account  shared a video purporting to show group  members killing one of their own with a  sledgehammer the victim had identified  himself as a former convict who tried to  switch sides and fight for Ukraine the  authenticity of that footage which we  are not showing here couldn't be  verified but progressions separately  said the man was a traitor Days Later  Wagner used a sledgehammer again this  time the group sent one to the European  Parliament after the EU branded Russia a  state sponsor of terrorism  the Russian Ministry of Defense didn't  respond to our request for comment on  Wagner's growing prominence in Ukraine  Wagner's rise comes at a time when  nationalist hardliners in Russia are  calling for tougher methods to turn the  tide in moscow's favor and the  mercenaries activities in Ukraine are  welcomed by pro-war critics of the  Kremlin  military analysts in the west say the  expanded role for an organization  accused of war crimes and one that is  recruiting convicted criminals is a  telling indicator of the perilous state  of Putin's War Machine  in December Putin said that the military  campaign in Ukraine was going well

